#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Portly shows which TCP ports are listening and which project each belongs to.

  portly               the live view in a terminal, else the printed list
  portly list          your dev servers, grouped per project, printed once
  portly -a            every listening port, desktop apps and helper processes too
  portly shop          only what belongs to shop (project, branch, folder or tool)
  portly 3000          one port in detail
  portly kill 3000     stop what listens on it: the process, or its container
  portly kill wt1      stop everything of a project or stack, after asking
  portly restart 3000  stop it and start it again the way it was started
  portly open 3000     open http://localhost:3000 in the browser
  portly logs 3000     follow its output: docker logs, or the file it writes to
  portly free 3000     the first free port from 3000 on
  portly wait 3000     wait until something listens on 3000
  portly --json        the same data as JSON, for scripts
  portly live          interactive: pick a port, see it, open, follow, copy or stop it
                       (what plain portly opens in a terminal)

A process belongs to the git checkout it runs from, so a worktree is a project
of its own next to the main checkout of its repo. A port that Docker forwards
is named after its container and belongs to the checkout of its compose
project, except a stack that wt started (compose project <name>-wt<n>): that
belongs to its worktree, found in wt's registry, .git/<name>-worktrees/<n>.env.
Colour is dropped with NO_COLOR or when the output is not a terminal.
"""
